Deal Intake Automation: Writeup

What works
Every 15 minutes the script reads Gmail threads labelled deal-intake. For each one it extracts deal fields from the attached deck (or the email body, if there is no deck), checks the company against the CRM, writes a new row if it is new, generates a summary Google Doc linked from that row, assigns an owner based on sector, and posts a notification. Failures are isolated per email: one bad deck cannot stop the run and every decision is logged to a Run_Log tab.
Dedupe: why domain, not name
A company controls its email domain. It does not control how people type its name — "Acme", "Acme Technologies" and "Acme.ai" all arrive from @acme.ai. So the domain is the primary key, normalized for case and subdomains.

Two cases break that, and both matter more than the happy path:

Free mail. Keying on gmail.com would reject every second founder pitching from a personal address as a duplicate of the first — a silent failure that loses deals with no trace. Free domains are therefore excluded from the key entirely and the row is flagged for review.

Name collisions. When the domain can't decide, a normalized name check runs — lowercased, with suffixes like "Technologies", "Labs" and "Pvt Ltd" stripped. But it flags rather than skips. Two different companies can share a name, and a false skip loses a deal permanently while a flagged row costs a partner ten seconds.

A live example from testing: a company pitching from a Gmail address with no deck, whose name matched an existing CRM entry, produced a row flagged no_deck_body_only; free_email_domain; possible_duplicate: name match "vendra" but different domain. Three subsystems degrading independently, and a human left with everything they need to decide.
Missing data
Seed decks routinely omit revenue. The model is instructed to return null for anything not stated and never to infer a number: an absent revenue figure must not become "early stage" or "0". A validation layer then converts every null to "not stated" and guarantees the row shape, so a sparse deck produces an honest thin row rather than a crash or an invented figure.

Tested against a real deck with no founder name stated: the row reads "not stated", not a guess. Rows missing five or more fields are flagged sparse_extraction so thin extractions are visible rather than quietly wrong.
Sector routing
The Sector_Owners tab is editable and is the source of truth. Rather than letting the model guess a sector and then mapping its guess, the tab's sector list is read into the prompt at request time as a closed set of options. The model chooses from Dexter's sectors or returns "Other"; adding a row to the tab expands what it can choose. Anything unmatched routes to Unassigned, because a deal waiting for a human beats a deal on the wrong partner's desk.
What I'd harden with more time
WhatsApp. Notifications currently post to a webhook (Google Chat/Slack). There is no clean free WhatsApp API, and I judged it a poor trade to spend extraction time on Twilio setup. It swaps into the notify() function alone: no other file knows how notifications are sent.

A third dedupe key. The founder's own email address, so the same person following up from a personal account is caught. Currently, that passes as new.

A review queue. Flagged rows sit inline in the CRM today. A separate tab holding them until someone confirms would scale better past a few hundred deals.

Follow-up decks. The script reads the first message in a thread. A deck sent in a later reply is missed.

Cost and volume logging. Per-run token spend, to make the economics visible before this runs on real inbound volume.
One honest limitation
Sector classification is fuzzy at the edges. A workwear rental company selling to hotels was classified Consumer; B2B is equally defensible. The system is built so this is cheap to fix rather than pretending it is solved, a partner corrects the row in seconds, and the mapping tab is theirs to edit.
